// =================== roadmap-schedule-leaf-expansion: decomposition -> leaf OCCURRENCES ===================
// A decomposition reads as leaf OCCURRENCES with the dependency EDGES between them: one occurrence per leaf
// at member 0, one per member of a produced/read family at its DECLARED count, and one per member of a
// FindingsOf family at a SIMULATION-input count -- an ASSUMED count the carrier itself declares none of.
// The edge relation is stage k depending on stage k-1, plus every waits_on row aimed into the child's own
// stage. The expansion WRITES NOTHING. It carries no order or date field of its own: the occurrences are an
// unordered set and the edges are the dependency relation, not a sequence -- an order or date field would
// appear only where a named consumer decides something from it (DESIGN.md *Plans are intentions*, ruling
// gunbc.design_ruling::planning_scheduling_is_cut). Its executing consumer today is the leaf-expansion witness
// (test.claim.roadmap.roadmap_schedule_leaf_expansion_witness), which reads the occurrences and their edges
// through the accessors below. Its only later consumer is the declared frontier roadmap-schedule-list-scheduling,
// declared in the approved roadmap gunb-ai/gunbc#16650, which reads expand_decomposition and these accessors and
// decides under its OWN ticket what to do with the occurrences; the trigger is that unit landing over this
// branch. This change builds no schedule, calendar, or in-flight set and claims none (out of scope). It mints no
// gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_authority node -- a leaf has no identity until claimed. Occurrences are keyed by the
// single carrier LeafPath (imported from gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_model above); nothing here re-spells that tuple.
//
// The expansion's ONE fail-closed gate is the carrier's OWN well-formedness claim,
// decomposition_dependencies_well_formed: a planted waits_on naming a stage the parent's decomposition does
// not have refuses there, and the expansion CALLS that claim rather than re-checking a row the carrier has
// already admitted.
//
// Indices are BUILT as `Nat` by successor (Zero / Succ) at each enumeration site, and a stage is walked by
// STRUCTURAL recursion over its own list (Cons / Empty) carrying a single Nat position -- the position a
// concept is modeled once (minimize_redundancy), never as a parallel Int counter that must be kept in step by
// hand. A family's members are walked DOWN by STRUCTURAL recursion on a `Nat` bound, just like the stage and
// piece lists, while the member index counts UP as the Nat the occurrence is keyed by -- how-many-left and
// which-one are distinct concepts, not one position twice. That Nat bound comes from the findings_count
// simulation input (already a Nat, so a negative assumed count is UNWRITABLE -- fail_closed prefers making the
// bad state unwritable to guarding it) or, for a DECLARED family, from the carrier's bare Int `population_members`
// (imported, never edited -- single_authority); that single Int is crossed into a Nat ONCE, inside `piece_occurrences`
// via the checked crossing `checked_int_to_nat`, whose Absent (a non-positive count) REFUSES as `OccsRejected` --
// the refusal is in the builder's own type, not a bool gate a caller could skip and not a fabricated zero. The
// expansion and the parent resolution both consume that typed `OccurrenceBuild`. So no UNCHECKED Int ever reaches
// a Nat field, and no malformed count is ever read as an accidental empty family. Dependencies and non-positive
// declared counts are refused by the typed carriers below, not by a mint-time check.

// The count an occurrence's member index is drawn from: DECLARED by the carrier, or ASSUMED (a simulation
// input the carrier never declared, used only for a FindingsOf family whose count is unknown until its
// probe runs). This is the carrier's single declared-vs-assumed distinction; a FindingsOf member is never
// DECLARED, so it is never offered as claimable.
type OccurrenceCount = Declared | Assumed

type Occurrence {
  path: LeafPath
  count_source: OccurrenceCount
}

// An edge between two occurrences: `to` waits on `from`.
type OccurrenceEdge {
  from: LeafPath
  to: LeafPath
}

// The three distinct reasons an expansion refuses, TYPED so a consumer matches rather than parsing prose
// (fail_closed: a wrong answer is a loud, typed, located error; source_annotations: a refusal / status / ruling
// belongs in a typed carrier). Each class names WHICH single authority was not satisfied and carries the offending
// node so the diagnostic is located: the carrier's dependency claim, a non-positive DECLARED family population, or
// a node that is not uniquely decomposed. "Not uniquely decomposed" is ONE concept whether it is the node being
// expanded or a waits_on parent, so a single variant names it (single_authority). No findings-count refusal exists:
// findings_count is a Nat, so a negative ASSUMED count is unwritable (fail_closed: making a bad state unwritable
// beats guarding it); only a DECLARED count -- the carrier's bare Int, imported not edited -- can be non-positive,
// and that is the refusal below.
type ExpansionRefusal =
    DependencyClaimRefused { item: RoadmapNodeId }
  | NonPositiveDeclaredCount { item: RoadmapNodeId }
  | NotUniquelyDecomposed { item: RoadmapNodeId }

// A top-level Composite piece that the expansion does NOT expand into occurrences -- the visible half of the
// (b) deferral. The carrier's OWN leaf-path authority (decomposition_has_leaf_path) REFUSES a path into a
// Composite: a Composite is a container addressed deeper than the path reaches, and LeafPath { item, stage, piece,
// member } carries no coordinate for a nested Composite stage. So a Composite's nested worker pieces, though they
// carry days, are not occurrences today -- that addressing is the LeafPath model change deferred to
// roadmap-schedule-composite-nested-addressing. The expansion does NOT silently undercount them (fail_closed: an
// omission is said, not hidden): it reports each top-level Composite keyed by the TOP-LEVEL piece's own LeafPath
// (no new path format) with its nested day total read from the ONE count helper piece_leaf_days. This is a REPORT,
// never a claim -- occurrence_is_offered_claimable admits only paths drawn from `occurrences`, never these.
// The nested day total is the SAME working-day prior as WorkPiece.days (read from the ONE count helper
// piece_leaf_days, which returns the same bare Int), held as a bare Int until the measure carrier grows.
// feature:working-day-prior-carrier -- dissolve-on: std.measure grows a working-day (effort) family.
// Owning lane: std.measure.
type UnexpandedComposite {
  path: LeafPath
  nested_days: Int
}

// A waits_on endpoint that is WELL-FORMED -- the carrier's OWN well-formedness claim already admitted the row
// (the stage index EXISTS on that endpoint's decomposition and the parent edge is DECLARED), so it is NOT a malformed
// row -- yet whose occurrence span AT OR BELOW the named stage mints NO occurrence, because every piece in that span
// is a Join, Decision or Composite: the three piece kinds that, by design, emit no occurrence. Such a wait is not a
// defect to refuse, it is CROSS-ITEM ORDERING onto a milestone or a project Join, which no lane schedules yet
// (out_of_scope of roadmap-schedule-barrier-through-empty-stages: "no cross-item ordering"). Refusing the whole item
// for it would blank the item's real work; dropping it silently would hide the gap. So it is REPORTED -- named and
// counted in the expansion's unresolved_waits, beside omitted_composites (fail_closed: an omission is SAID, not
// hidden) -- and the item still expands. A genuinely MALFORMED row (a stage index that does not exist, or a parent
// the item does not declare) is NOT this: the carrier's well-formedness claim refuses it as DependencyClaimRefused
// BEFORE any of this runs, which is the pre-#16716 sense and the #16678 red control, kept intact.
type WaitSide = From | Into

// The piece kinds the empty span held, so a reader sees WHY no occurrence emitted (a Join, a Decision, a Composite)
// rather than an unexplained silence. Only these three kinds ever appear here -- a span that minted an occurrence is
// never unresolved -- but the producer states what it saw instead of asserting it, so nothing is fabricated.
type UnresolvedWaitReason = EndpointEmitsNoOccurrence { kinds: List<PieceKind> }

type UnresolvedWait {
  row: StageDependency
  side: WaitSide
  stage: Int
  reason: UnresolvedWaitReason
}

// The expansion is fail-closed: it either holds the occurrences, their edges, the top-level Composites it did
// NOT expand, and the waits_on rows it could NOT attach (so a schedule over an item with Composites or with a wait on
// an occurrence-less milestone says what it omits or could not order), or a typed, located refusal for a malformed
// row / bad count / non-unique node.
type Expansion
  = Expanded { occurrences: List<Occurrence>, edges: List<OccurrenceEdge>, omitted_composites: List<UnexpandedComposite>, unresolved_waits: List<UnresolvedWait> }
  | ExpansionRejected { cause: ExpansionRefusal }

// Members: emit `remaining` occurrences with the given count source. `remaining` is a Nat walked DOWN by
// structural recursion (Zero / Succ), exactly like the stage and piece walkers -- there is no Int loop bound to
// guard and no `<= 0` arm that could silently drop a malformed (negative) count. The member index counts UP as
// the Nat key, built by successor; the two are distinct concepts, not one position tracked twice.
fn members_occurrences(item: RoadmapNodeId, stage: Nat, piece: Nat, remaining: Nat, index: Nat, source: OccurrenceCount) -> List<Occurrence> {
  match remaining {
    Zero => []
    Succ { prev: k } => concat(
      [ member_occurrence(item: item, stage: stage, piece: piece, member: index, source: source) ],
      members_occurrences(item: item, stage: stage, piece: piece, remaining: k, index: Succ { prev: index }, source: source),
    )
  }
}

// A declared family's member count reaches the member walk through a checked Int->Nat crossing, so building a
// piece's occurrences can REFUSE: this is the ONE place that crossing (and its refusal) lives. There is no
// separate bool gate a caller might skip and no fabricated default -- a non-positive declared population is
// OccsRejected, located at the offending item (fail_closed: "a silent widen, fabricated default ... is a hard
// reject"; every path succeeds fully or fails typed/located). A FindingsOf family is exempt -- its count is the
// Nat findings_count simulation input, whose negative is unwritable, so it never crosses and never refuses.
type OccurrenceBuild
  = OccsBuilt { occurrences: List<Occurrence> }
  | OccsRejected { item: RoadmapNodeId }

// THE single place an Occurrence is minted. Every index it stamps arrives as a `Nat` already built by successor
// (Zero / Succ) at the enumeration sites below. The one Int->Nat crossing -- the declared family count -- is the
// CHECKED one in `piece_occurrences`, and its result is a Nat loop bound consumed by `members_occurrences`, not a
// value handed straight to this mint; so the mint itself sees only successor-built Nats, there is no
// representability branch to bypass, and the mint is total.
fn member_occurrence(item: RoadmapNodeId, stage: Nat, piece: Nat, member: Nat, source: OccurrenceCount) -> Occurrence {
  Occurrence { path: LeafPath { item: item, stage: stage, piece: piece, member: member }, count_source: source }
}

// The single authority for crossing a DECLARED family's carrier Int into the Nat the member walk consumes. A
// declared family must carry at least ONE member, so only a STRICTLY positive count is admissible: this returns
// Absent for a non-positive one (a negative is the checked crossing's own Absent; zero is a declared family with
// no member, a contradiction -- unlike the Nat FindingsOf input where zero legitimately means "no findings yet")
// and the positive Nat otherwise. The builder refuses on Absent rather than minting zero (fail_closed: the
// reviewer's "return Optional<Nat>, refuse on Absent," refined so a zero declared count is refused too). One
// crossing, one admissibility rule, located refusal -- no fabricated default, no separate bool gate to skip.
fn declared_member_nat(n: Int) -> Optional<Nat> {
  match checked_int_to_nat(n: n) {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: k } => match k {
      Zero => Absent
      Succ { prev: m } => Present { value: Succ { prev: m } }
    }
  }
}

// One piece -> its occurrences, or a located refusal. The FindingsOf branch mints from the Nat simulation input
// directly; every OTHER piece is total (a leaf, or a join/decision/composite that carries no member). Only the
// DECLARED family branch reads the carrier's bare Int `population_members` and must CROSS it into the Nat the
// member walk consumes -- via the ONE checked crossing `declared_member_nat`, whose Absent (a non-positive count)
// REFUSES here rather than minting zero. This is the sole Int->Nat crossing in the enumeration; the mint itself
// (`member_occurrence`) never sees an unchecked Int.
fn piece_occurrences(item: RoadmapNodeId, stage: Nat, piece: Nat, p: WorkPiece, findings_count: Nat) -> OccurrenceBuild {
  match p {
    Leaf { label, days, evidence } => OccsBuilt { occurrences: [ member_occurrence(item: item, stage: stage, piece: piece, member: Zero, source: Declared) ] }
    Family { label, days, population, owner } =>
      if population_is_findings(p: population) {
        OccsBuilt { occurrences: members_occurrences(item: item, stage: stage, piece: piece, remaining: findings_count, index: Zero, source: Assumed) }
      } else {
        match declared_member_nat(n: population_members(p: population)) {
          Present { value: k } => OccsBuilt { occurrences: members_occurrences(item: item, stage: stage, piece: piece, remaining: k, index: Zero, source: Declared) }
          Absent => OccsRejected { item: item }
        }
      }
    Join { label } => OccsBuilt { occurrences: [] }
    Decision { label, alternatives } => OccsBuilt { occurrences: [] }
    Composite { label, stages } => OccsBuilt { occurrences: [] }
  }
}

// Walk a stage's pieces by STRUCTURAL recursion (Cons / Empty), carrying the single Nat piece index that keys
// each occurrence -- no parallel Int counter, no index arithmetic (minimize_redundancy). A refused piece
// (non-positive declared count) propagates the located refusal; otherwise the built lists concatenate.
fn stage_pieces_occurrences(item: RoadmapNodeId, stage: Nat, pieces: List<WorkPiece>, piece: Nat, findings_count: Nat) -> OccurrenceBuild {
  match pieces {
    Empty => OccsBuilt { occurrences: [] }
    Cons { head: p, tail: rest } =>
      match piece_occurrences(item: item, stage: stage, piece: piece, p: p, findings_count: findings_count) {
        OccsRejected { item: bad } => OccsRejected { item: bad }
        OccsBuilt { occurrences: head_occs } =>
          match stage_pieces_occurrences(item: item, stage: stage, pieces: rest, piece: Succ { prev: piece }, findings_count: findings_count) {
            OccsRejected { item: bad } => OccsRejected { item: bad }
            OccsBuilt { occurrences: tail_occs } => OccsBuilt { occurrences: concat(head_occs, tail_occs) }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn stage_occurrences(item: RoadmapNodeId, stage: Nat, st: WorkStage, findings_count: Nat) -> OccurrenceBuild {
  stage_pieces_occurrences(item: item, stage: stage, pieces: st.pieces, piece: Zero, findings_count: findings_count)
}

// Walk a decomposition's stages by structural recursion, carrying the single Nat stage index; a refused piece in
// any stage propagates the located refusal.
fn stages_occurrences(item: RoadmapNodeId, stages: List<WorkStage>, stage: Nat, findings_count: Nat) -> OccurrenceBuild {
  match stages {
    Empty => OccsBuilt { occurrences: [] }
    Cons { head: st, tail: rest } =>
      match stage_occurrences(item: item, stage: stage, st: st, findings_count: findings_count) {
        OccsRejected { item: bad } => OccsRejected { item: bad }
        OccsBuilt { occurrences: head_occs } =>
          match stages_occurrences(item: item, stages: rest, stage: Succ { prev: stage }, findings_count: findings_count) {
            OccsRejected { item: bad } => OccsRejected { item: bad }
            OccsBuilt { occurrences: tail_occs } => OccsBuilt { occurrences: concat(head_occs, tail_occs) }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn all_occurrences(d: WorkDecomposition, findings_count: Nat) -> OccurrenceBuild {
  stages_occurrences(item: d.node, stages: d.stages, stage: Zero, findings_count: findings_count)
}

// Group the occurrences by stage ONCE, into a total function stage -> occurrences. Both edge builders below look
// a stage up here instead of scanning the whole occurrence list per occurrence / per row: the old
// occurrences_at_stage was a full filter invoked inside an outer scan, a proven quadratic fold that DESIGN.md
// *How to work* says is ALWAYS fixed regardless of realized n ("a copied accumulator, a quadratic fold ... n is
// small here is not time-stable"). Building the map is one pass; each new member is PREPENDED with
// concat([o], prev) -- concat copies only the one-element left list, never the growing group -- so the build is
// not a copied-accumulator fold either. Member order is reversed by prepending; occurrence order is
// order-insensitive here (the witness checks counts and per-edge endpoints, not sequence).
fn occurrences_by_stage(occs: List<Occurrence>) -> Map<Int, List<Occurrence>> {
  let groups: Map<Int, List<Occurrence>> = fold(occs, init: empty_map(), f: fn(m, o) {
    match map_lookup(m: m, key: nat_to_int(n: o.path.stage)) {
      Present { value: prev } => map_insert(m: m, key: nat_to_int(n: o.path.stage), value: concat([o], prev))
      Absent => map_insert(m: m, key: nat_to_int(n: o.path.stage), value: [o])
    }
  })
  groups
}

// THE single place the "holds no single decomposition" rule is computed -- the same rule decomposition_has_leaf_path
// applies to a path. Both the waits_on parent resolution and expand_node consume this one result rather than each
// re-deriving `count(decomposition_for(..)) == 1`, so the concept is not forked into a second copy (single_authority).
// It yields the node's one decomposition, or a typed NotUniquelyDecomposed located at the node.
type UniqueDecomposition
  = UniqueFound { d: WorkDecomposition }
  | UniqueRejected { cause: ExpansionRefusal }

fn unique_decomposition(item: RoadmapNodeId) -> UniqueDecomposition {
  let ds = decomposition_for(node: item)
  if count(ds) == 1 {
    match ds[0] {
      Present { value: d } => UniqueFound { d: d }
      Absent => UniqueRejected { cause: NotUniquelyDecomposed { item: item } }
    }
  } else {
    UniqueRejected { cause: NotUniquelyDecomposed { item: item } }
  }
}

// A waits_on parent is resolved ONCE: its occurrences at the waited-from Int stage, built exactly like every
// other occurrence over the parent's OWN decomposition (so an edge endpoint is a real occurrence path, never a
// synthetic mint -- single_authority), or a typed Rejected. The uniqueness rule and the non-positive
// declared-count rule are the SAME two the top-level expansion applies -- resolve_parent_occurrences consumes
// those single authorities (unique_decomposition, and the typed OccurrenceBuild from all_occurrences) rather than
// re-checking rows -- so a waits_on parent is admitted under exactly the child's rules and a bad parent count is
// REFUSED, located at the parent, never silently dropped to "no edges".
type ParentResolution
  = Resolved { occurrences: List<Occurrence> }
  | EmitsNoOccurrence { kinds: List<PieceKind> }
  | Rejected { cause: ExpansionRefusal }

// A stage emits an occurrence EXACTLY when `groups` holds a key for it -- occurrences_by_stage inserts a key only
// for a stage that produced at least one occurrence -- so a Present lookup IS the non-emptiness test. These two
// walkers find, for a named stage, the nearest stage AT OR BELOW it that emits an occurrence, by STRUCTURAL descent
// on Nat (Zero / Succ), one map lookup per step, bounded by the stage count (a modeling constant -- the same
// bounded-scan class DESIGN.md *How to work* accepts for the parent filter). No Int loop, no sort, no list scan.
fn nearest_nonempty_stage(groups: Map<Int, List<Occurrence>>, k: Nat) -> Int? {
  match map_lookup(m: groups, key: nat_to_int(n: k)) {
    Present { value: _ } => Present { value: nat_to_int(n: k) }
    Absent => match k {
      Zero => Absent
      Succ { prev: j } => nearest_nonempty_stage(groups: groups, k: j)
    }
  }
}

// The same walk entered from a bare Int stage index: cross it into Nat through the ONE checked crossing; a
// negative (or a stage with no non-empty stage at or below it) is Absent, which the callers now RECORD as an
// unresolved wait (not a refusal), so an unresolvable wait is still said, never dropped silently (fail_closed).
fn nearest_nonempty_at_or_below(groups: Map<Int, List<Occurrence>>, named: Int) -> Int? {
  match checked_int_to_nat(n: named) {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: k } => nearest_nonempty_stage(groups: groups, k: k)
  }
}

// The ONE projection of a top-level WorkPiece to its PieceKind. The match is TOTAL over the coproduct -- if a variant
// is ever added to, or renamed in, WorkPiece this fails to compile, which is the point: the report can never carry a
// kind the coproduct does not have. Only Join / Decision / Composite can ever reach an unresolved-wait report (a span
// that minted an occurrence is never unresolved), but the match names all five so the report states what it actually
// saw rather than special-casing.
fn piece_kind(p: WorkPiece) -> PieceKind {
  match p {
    Leaf { label: _, days: _, evidence: _ } => LeafKind
    Family { label: _, days: _, population: _, owner: _ } => FamilyKind
    Join { label: _ } => JoinKind
    Decision { label: _, alternatives: _ } => DecisionKind
    Composite { label: _, stages: _ } => CompositeKind
  }
}

// The ONE comparator for PieceKind -- an exhaustive cross-match, so equality never leans on a derived String form.
fn piece_kinds_equal(a: PieceKind, b: PieceKind) -> Bool {
  match a {
    LeafKind => match b { LeafKind => true FamilyKind => false JoinKind => false DecisionKind => false CompositeKind => false }
    FamilyKind => match b { LeafKind => false FamilyKind => true JoinKind => false DecisionKind => false CompositeKind => false }
    JoinKind => match b { LeafKind => false FamilyKind => false JoinKind => true DecisionKind => false CompositeKind => false }
    DecisionKind => match b { LeafKind => false FamilyKind => false JoinKind => false DecisionKind => true CompositeKind => false }
    CompositeKind => match b { LeafKind => false FamilyKind => false JoinKind => false DecisionKind => false CompositeKind => true }
  }
}

// The single membership test a reader (or a witness) uses against a reported `kinds`, so no caller re-derives equality.
fn has_kind(ks: List<PieceKind>, k: PieceKind) -> Bool {
  count(filter(ks, e => piece_kinds_equal(a: e, b: k))) > 0
}

fn stage_kinds(st: WorkStage) -> List<PieceKind> {
  map(st.pieces, piece_kind)
}

// The distinct piece kinds across the TOP-LEVEL pieces of stages 0..named. Entered only for an endpoint already
// known to emit no occurrence (named >= 0 is guaranteed by the carrier's well-formedness gate), so it walks DOWN the
// list by structural recursion (Empty stops it) with an Int budget counting DOWN -- bounded, no unguarded loop. The
// kinds are de-duplicated by first appearance so the report reads as a small set, not a repetition.
fn span_kinds(stages: List<WorkStage>, named: Int) -> List<PieceKind> {
  distinct_kinds(span_kinds_at(stages: stages, remaining: named))
}

fn span_kinds_at(stages: List<WorkStage>, remaining: Int) -> List<PieceKind> {
  if remaining < 0 {
    []
  } else {
    match stages {
      Empty => []
      Cons { head: st, tail: rest } => concat(stage_kinds(st), span_kinds_at(stages: rest, remaining: remaining - 1))
    }
  }
}

fn distinct_kinds(ks: List<PieceKind>) -> List<PieceKind> {
  fold(ks, init: [], f: fn(acc, k) {
    if count(filter(acc, e => piece_kinds_equal(a: e, b: k))) == 0 { concat(acc, [k]) } else { acc }
  })
}

// A row's PARENT endpoint is resolved to a stage that actually emits the parent's occurrences: the named from_stage
// if the parent emits there, else its nearest non-empty PREDECESSOR (a barrier that carries across an empty stage).
// When NO stage at or below from_stage emits an occurrence the row is NOT refused -- the parent (a milestone Join or a
// lone Composite) is a declared, in-range endpoint, so it is a WAIT ON SOMETHING THAT EMITS NOTHING (EmitsNoOccurrence),
// which the caller records as an unresolved wait rather than blanking the item. A genuine Rejected here means a real
// defect: the parent is not uniquely decomposed, or a DECLARED family on it carries a non-positive count (fail_closed).
fn resolve_parent_occurrences(item: RoadmapNodeId, from_stage: Int, findings_count: Nat) -> ParentResolution {
  match unique_decomposition(item: item) {
    UniqueRejected { cause } => Rejected { cause: cause }
    UniqueFound { d: pd } =>
      match all_occurrences(d: pd, findings_count: findings_count) {
        OccsRejected { item: bad } => Rejected { cause: NonPositiveDeclaredCount { item: bad } }
        OccsBuilt { occurrences: occs } => resolve_from_parent_occurrences(item: item, occs: occs, from_stage: from_stage, stages: pd.stages)
      }
  }
}

// The parent's built occurrence list, grouped ONCE at the top (no let inside a match arm), resolves one row's
// from_stage endpoint to the nearest non-empty stage at or below it (a barrier carried across an empty stage) or,
// when none exists, reports the endpoint as emitting no occurrence (the kinds it held), which the caller records as an
// unresolved From wait -- never a silent drop and never a whole-item refusal (fail_closed: the gap is SAID).
fn resolve_from_parent_occurrences(item: RoadmapNodeId, occs: List<Occurrence>, from_stage: Int, stages: List<WorkStage>) -> ParentResolution {
  let pgroups: Map<Int, List<Occurrence>> = occurrences_by_stage(occs: occs)
  match nearest_nonempty_at_or_below(groups: pgroups, named: from_stage) {
    Absent => EmitsNoOccurrence { kinds: span_kinds(stages: stages, named: from_stage) }
    Present { value: j } =>
      match map_lookup(m: pgroups, key: j) {
        Absent => EmitsNoOccurrence { kinds: span_kinds(stages: stages, named: from_stage) }
        Present { value: ps } => Resolved { occurrences: ps }
      }
  }
}

// STAGE BARRIER: each occurrence in stage k waits on EVERY occurrence in the NEAREST NON-EMPTY stage below it. The
// old code linked stage k to stage k-1 only (a single map lookup on `stage - 1`), so when k-1 emitted no occurrence
// -- a Decision/Join/Composite-only stage -- stage k waited on NOTHING and the barrier silently vanished. The real
// defect (factory-attempt-execution-on-fabric-lease): 8 audit leaves in stage 0, only a Decision in stage 1, 8 cut
// leaves in stage 2, and ZERO audit-to-cut edges emitted, so the scheduler let cut start before audit. Now stage k
// descends to its nearest non-empty EARLIER stage, so the barrier CARRIES THROUGH empty stages. The barrier is
// between the STAGES, so it crosses their whole occurrence sets (one edge per (lower, upper) pair, not one per upper
// leaf; an upper leaf waiting on only one of its stage's predecessors could start before its own stage is done).
// Stage 0 has no earlier stage and so waits on nothing -- correct, not an omission. The group is one map lookup and
// the descent is bounded by the stage count (a modeling constant); nothing scans the whole list per occurrence.
fn stage_barrier_edges(occs: List<Occurrence>) -> List<OccurrenceEdge> {
  let groups: Map<Int, List<Occurrence>> = occurrences_by_stage(occs: occs)
  flat_map(occs, o =>
    match o.path.stage {
      Zero => []
      Succ { prev: below } =>
        match nearest_nonempty_stage(groups: groups, k: below) {
          Absent => []
          Present { value: j } =>
            match map_lookup(m: groups, key: j) {
              Absent => []
              Present { value: lower } => map(lower, u => OccurrenceEdge { from: u.path, to: o.path })
            }
        }
    }
  )
}


// One declared stage-dependency row, folded into either its (resolved) edge set or a located refusal.
type WaitsEdges
  = WaitsBuilt { edges: List<OccurrenceEdge>, unresolved: List<UnresolvedWait> }
  | WaitsRejected { cause: ExpansionRefusal }

// Stage-level dependency: every occurrence of the parent's resolved FROM stage waits on every occurrence of this
// decomposition's resolved INTO stage -- the rows are declared once per (parent, stage) pair, not per leaf pair. BOTH
// endpoints carry through an empty stage to their nearest non-empty predecessor. An endpoint whose whole span
// at-or-below the named stage is WELL-FORMED (in range, edge declared -- the carrier's claim admitted it) yet mints NO
// occurrence (only Join/Decision/Composite) is NOT a refusal: a wait onto such a milestone/Join is cross-item ordering
// no lane schedules yet, so it is RECORDed as an UnresolvedWait on the failing side and contributes no edge, and the
// item still expands -- named and counted, never a silent drop (fail_closed). A row whose endpoints both resolve
// contributes its cross-product edges; a genuine Rejected from the parent (not-unique / non-positive declared count)
// still refuses the whole expansion. The child groups are built ONCE, outside the fold; rows are PREPENDED.
fn waits_on_edges(d: WorkDecomposition, occs: List<Occurrence>, findings_count: Nat) -> WaitsEdges {
  let groups: Map<Int, List<Occurrence>> = occurrences_by_stage(occs: occs)
  fold(d.waits_on, init: WaitsBuilt { edges: [], unresolved: [] }, f: fn(acc, w) {
    match acc {
      WaitsRejected { cause } => acc
      WaitsBuilt { edges, unresolved } => match resolve_parent_occurrences(item: w.item, from_stage: w.from_stage, findings_count: findings_count) {
        Rejected { cause } => WaitsRejected { cause: cause }
        EmitsNoOccurrence { kinds } =>
          WaitsBuilt { edges: edges, unresolved: concat([ UnresolvedWait { row: w, side: From, stage: w.from_stage, reason: EndpointEmitsNoOccurrence { kinds: kinds } } ], unresolved) }
        Resolved { occurrences: ps } => match nearest_nonempty_at_or_below(groups: groups, named: w.into_stage) {
          Absent =>
            WaitsBuilt { edges: edges, unresolved: concat([ UnresolvedWait { row: w, side: Into, stage: w.into_stage, reason: EndpointEmitsNoOccurrence { kinds: span_kinds(stages: d.stages, named: w.into_stage) } } ], unresolved) }
          Present { value: j } => match map_lookup(m: groups, key: j) {
            Absent =>
              WaitsBuilt { edges: edges, unresolved: concat([ UnresolvedWait { row: w, side: Into, stage: w.into_stage, reason: EndpointEmitsNoOccurrence { kinds: span_kinds(stages: d.stages, named: w.into_stage) } } ], unresolved) }
            Present { value: cs } =>
              WaitsBuilt { edges: concat(flat_map(cs, c => map(ps, p => OccurrenceEdge { from: p.path, to: c.path })), edges), unresolved: unresolved }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  })
}

// THE REPORT of the (b) deferral: the top-level Composite pieces the expansion does NOT expand into occurrences.
// A Composite is a container addressed deeper than LeafPath reaches (see the UnexpandedComposite note above), so its
// nested worker pieces are NOT occurrences today -- but an expansion must not quietly undercount them (fail_closed:
// "no plausible output is fabricated"; an omission is SAID, not hidden). This walks the TOP-LEVEL stage/piece grid by
// the SAME structural recursion the occurrence walk uses (Cons / Empty, carrying the single Nat stage and piece
// index), and for each top-level Composite mints an UnexpandedComposite keyed by that piece's OWN LeafPath (no new
// path format) with its nested day total read from the ONE count helper `piece_leaf_days`. A Composite nested inside a
// Composite is folded into its top-level ancestor's day total, not reported separately -- the report is per TOP-LEVEL
// piece. It is a REPORT only: nothing here mints an Occurrence or an OccurrenceEdge, and
// occurrence_is_offered_claimable can never admit one of these paths (it draws only from `occurrences`). Linear in
// the top-level piece count -- one pass, each hit PREPENDED via concat of a one-element list (never the growing list).
fn composite_reports(item: RoadmapNodeId, stages: List<WorkStage>, stage: Nat) -> List<UnexpandedComposite> {
  match stages {
    Empty => []
    Cons { head: st, tail: rest } => concat(
      stage_composite_reports(item: item, stage: stage, st: st),
      composite_reports(item: item, stages: rest, stage: Succ { prev: stage }),
    )
  }
}

// Read the top-level piece list off a TYPED WorkStage parameter (the SAME access stage_occurrences proves), then
// walk it by structural recursion carrying the single Nat piece index.
fn stage_composite_reports(item: RoadmapNodeId, stage: Nat, st: WorkStage) -> List<UnexpandedComposite> {
  piece_list_composite_reports(item: item, stage: stage, pieces: st.pieces, piece: Zero)
}

fn piece_list_composite_reports(item: RoadmapNodeId, stage: Nat, pieces: List<WorkPiece>, piece: Nat) -> List<UnexpandedComposite> {
  match pieces {
    Empty => []
    Cons { head: p, tail: rest } => concat(
      piece_composite_report(item: item, stage: stage, piece: piece, p: p),
      piece_list_composite_reports(item: item, stage: stage, pieces: rest, piece: Succ { prev: piece }),
    )
  }
}

// One top-level piece -> a one-element report if it is a Composite, else nothing. A TYPED WorkPiece parameter
// (the SAME access piece_occurrences proves); a Composite nested inside a Composite is folded into its top-level
// ancestor's nested_days via piece_leaf_days, never reported separately. The hit is a one-element list PREPENDED
// via concat (linear: only the one-element left list is copied), never an accumulator append.
fn piece_composite_report(item: RoadmapNodeId, stage: Nat, piece: Nat, p: WorkPiece) -> List<UnexpandedComposite> {
  match p {
    Composite { label, stages } => [ UnexpandedComposite { path: LeafPath { item: item, stage: stage, piece: piece, member: Zero }, nested_days: piece_leaf_days(p: p) } ]
    _ => []
  }
}

// THE expansion, gated in one place over a single fully admitted decomposition. Order: (1) ask the carrier's OWN
// well-formedness claim (fail_closed, single_authority) -- a stage dependency naming a stage its parent's
// decomposition lacks, or an undeclared edge, refuses with the typed DependencyClaimRefused and re-checks no row;
// (2) build the occurrences -- a non-positive DECLARED family count refuses, located, from the builder itself
// (the assumed FindingsOf count is a Nat, its negative unwritable, so it never refuses); (3) each waits_on parent
// is resolved under the SAME rules (unique_decomposition + the typed build). Only over a fully admitted
// decomposition does it emit occurrences and edges. No branch here mints from an un-guarded count.
fn expand_decomposition(d: WorkDecomposition, findings_count: Nat) -> Expansion {
  if !decomposition_dependencies_well_formed(d: d) {
    ExpansionRejected { cause: DependencyClaimRefused { item: d.node } }
  } else {
    match all_occurrences(d: d, findings_count: findings_count) {
      OccsRejected { item: bad } => ExpansionRejected { cause: NonPositiveDeclaredCount { item: bad } }
      OccsBuilt { occurrences: occs } =>
        match waits_on_edges(d: d, occs: occs, findings_count: findings_count) {
          WaitsRejected { cause } => ExpansionRejected { cause: cause }
          WaitsBuilt { edges, unresolved } =>
            Expanded {
              occurrences: occs,
              edges: concat(stage_barrier_edges(occs: occs), edges),
              omitted_composites: composite_reports(item: d.node, stages: d.stages, stage: Zero),
              unresolved_waits: unresolved,
            }
        }
    }
  }
}

// Expand the decomposition carried for `item`: resolve its single decomposition through the SAME rule a waits_on
// parent uses (single_authority), then expand. Refuses with a typed, located cause if the node holds none or more
// than one.
fn expand_node(item: RoadmapNodeId, findings_count: Nat) -> Expansion {
  match unique_decomposition(item: item) {
    UniqueRejected { cause } => ExpansionRejected { cause: cause }
    UniqueFound { d: d } => expand_decomposition(d: d, findings_count: findings_count)
  }
}

// An occurrence is OFFERED CLAIMABLE under a policy DISTINCT from a mere count test: it may be offered
// only when its count is DECLARED *and* its path actually resolves under the carrier's own
// decomposition_has_leaf_path. It composes the count_source test with existing authority (the carrier's
// leaf predicate) rather than restating it -- an ASSUMED FindingsOf member is not declared (so not offered),
// and a declared member whose index the carrier does not acknowledge is likewise not offered.
// The count_source is matched directly here and in the witness: a two-case type needs no is_* wrapper.
fn occurrence_is_offered_claimable(o: Occurrence) -> Bool {
  o.count_source == Declared && decomposition_has_leaf_path(path: o.path)
}

// ---- The priced prior of one occurrence (roadmap-schedule-list-scheduling, gunb-ai/gunbc#16650)
//
// A schedule needs the WORKING-DAY prior an occurrence carries. The days live in the decomposition (WorkPiece.days
// is the single authority for them); this only LOOKS UP the prior a member path carries and answers Absent when the
// path has no duration to give: a non-leaf (Join / Decision / Composite), a leaf asked for anything but member 0, a
// bounded family whose member index its population does not hold, or a stage / piece that is not there. Answering
// Present { value: 0 } for any of those would mint a zero-day leaf for a path that carries no duration -- a
// fabricated default and a plausible output invented where the real answer is a refusal
// (@ref(gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed): "no plausible output is fabricated"; "A diff landing a silent widen,
// fabricated default ... is a hard reject"). Refusing is the consumer's job; this function's job is to answer
// Absent, never a made-up day count.
//
// The shape follows decomposition_has_leaf_path exactly -- the same Nat indexes crossed once via nat_to_int, the
// same list reads, the same arm-by-arm answer -- so the two cannot drift on WHICH path carries a duration: that
// function answers whether a worker may CLAIM the leaf, this one answers what a SIMULATION pays for it.
// A FindingsOf family is the one arm the two answer differently, and deliberately: decomposition_has_leaf_path
// refuses a findings member because no such leaf exists to claim until the probe produces it, while an expansion
// emits those very members as occurrences with count_source Assumed -- a declared simulation input
// (@ref(gunbc.design_ruling::schedule_answers_are_computed_cached_and_re_read): the answer is computed over the
// carriers, and an Assumed occurrence is scheduled with its source kept visible). Here the findings member index is
// NOT bounded by population_members (which answers 1 for a FindingsOf carrier, a count the probe does not hold);
// it is bounded by the member set the expansion itself emitted, which the scheduler checks against its own
// occurrence set (MemberIndexBeyondExpansion) instead of here. A Produced or Reading family IS bounded by its
// declared count in both.

fn piece_member_days(d: WorkDecomposition, path: LeafPath) -> Optional<Int> {
  let stage_index: Int = nat_to_int(n: path.stage)
  let piece_index: Int = nat_to_int(n: path.piece)
  let member_index: Int = nat_to_int(n: path.member)
  match d.stages[stage_index] {
    Absent => Absent
    Present { value: st } => match st.pieces[piece_index] {
      Absent => Absent
      Present { value: pc } => match pc {
        Leaf { label, days, evidence } => if member_index == 0 { Present { value: days } } else { Absent }
        Family { label, days, population, owner } =>
          if population_is_findings(p: population) {
            Present { value: days }
          } else if member_index < population_members(p: population) {
            Present { value: days }
          } else {
            Absent
          }
        Join { label } => Absent
        Decision { label, alternatives } => Absent
        Composite { label, stages } => Absent
      }
    }
  }
}
